## 5. Data Models

### 5.1 Trek Data (`src/types/trek.ts`)

```typescript
export type DifficultyLevel = 'easy' | 'moderate' | 'challenging' | 'strenuous';

export interface TrekItineraryDay {
  day: number;
  titleEn: string;
  titleZh: string;
  descriptionEn: string;
  descriptionZh: string;
  altitude?: number;        // meters
  walkingHours?: number;
}

export interface Trek {
  slug: string;              // URL-safe identifier, e.g. "everest-base-camp"
  nameEn: string;
  nameZh: string;
  descriptionEn: string;
  descriptionZh: string;
  difficulty: DifficultyLevel;
  durationDays: number;
  maxAltitude: number;       // meters
  priceUSD: number;
  bestSeasons: string[];     // e.g. ["Mar-May", "Sep-Nov"]
  bestSeasonsZh: string[];   // e.g. ["3月-5月", "9月-11月"]
  heroImage: string;         // path in /public/images/treks/
  images: string[];          // gallery images for this trek
  featured: boolean;         // show on homepage
  itinerary: TrekItineraryDay[];
  includedEn: string[];      // what's included list
  includedZh: string[];
  highlightsEn: string[];
  highlightsZh: string[];
}
```

**Design decision**: Trek data is stored in a TypeScript file (`src/data/treks.ts`) rather than in the dictionaries. This is because treks have structured fields (numeric altitude, prices, images, slugs) that don't fit cleanly into a flat translation JSON. The bilingual text fields (`nameEn`/`nameZh`, etc.) are embedded directly in the data objects. A helper function `getTrekField(trek, field, lang)` can extract the correct language variant.

### 5.2 Trek Data File (`src/data/treks.ts`)

This file exports an array of `Trek` objects. The individual trek pages use `generateStaticParams()` to produce all `/en/treks/[slug]` and `/zh/treks/[slug]` combinations:

```typescript
// In src/app/[lang]/treks/[slug]/page.tsx
export async function generateStaticParams() {
  const locales = i18n.locales;
  return treks.flatMap((trek) =>
    locales.map((lang) => ({ lang, slug: trek.slug }))
  );
}
```

Wait -- actually, since `[lang]` is a parent dynamic segment with its own `generateStaticParams`, and `[slug]` is a child, Next.js composes them automatically. The `[slug]` page only needs to return `slug` values:

```typescript
// In src/app/[lang]/treks/[slug]/page.tsx
export async function generateStaticParams() {
  return treks.map((trek) => ({ slug: trek.slug }));
}
```

Next.js will cross-product this with the parent `[lang]` params.

